.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-catalog-rebuild 1  "tapectl-catalog-rebuild " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-catalog\-rebuild \- Reconstruct catalog rows by reading a sealed volume — the path back when the database is gone and there is no backup
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl catalog rebuild\fR [\fB\-\-from\-volume\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] <\fB\-\-key\fR> [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-label\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-tenant\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reconstruct catalog rows by reading a sealed volume — the path back when the database is gone and there is no backup
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-volume\fR
Rebuild from a sealed tape volume. The only source today; named rather than implied so a later `\-\-from\-export` is additive
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path). Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured.

There is still no `/dev/nst0` fallback (ADR\-0010): `/dev/nstN` numbering is not stable across reboots on a host with more than one drive, and unlike a read\-only dump this command writes what it reads into the catalog — a silent wrong\-device default would file one tape\*(Aqs contents under another\*(Aqs. The sole configured backend\*(Aqs `device_tape` is not a guess about which drive you meant; a guessed device number is. Resolve by serial through `/dev/tape/by\-id/`.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fI<KEY>\fR
Operator or escrow secret key file. A tenant key cannot open the operator envelope and is refused with a pointer at RESTORE.sh
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-label\fR \fI<LABEL>\fR
Refuse unless the tape\*(Aqs own reported label matches — a wrong\-tape guard, not a database lookup
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept (issue #147)
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, receipts. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home (issue #109).
.TP
\fB\-\-tenant\fR \fI<TENANT>\fR [default: recovered]
Tenant to file units under when no tenant envelope on this cartridge names their owner (only reachable with a damaged or partial tape)
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, receipts, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME (issue #109).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
